#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186114944 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ef75bc93-76da-5992-8285-181b808eb7a0" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Range of a Transformed Exponential Function"}] :question/id #uuid "79b049d8-e6ca-5c89-8e8d-89e937021df9" :question/math-academy-id "e-4118" :question/problem "What is the range of $y = 2(4)^x+3$?" :question/worked-solution "The range of $4^x$ is\n\n$$\n4^x \\gt 0\n$$\n\nMultiplying the above inequality by $2$ and then adding $3$, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}4^{x} & > 0 \\\\ 2(4)^{x} & > 0 \\\\ 2(4)^{x} + 3 & > 0 + 3 \\\\ y & > 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the range is $y \\in (3,\\infty)$.\n\nWe can also see that this is true from the graph of the function. The graph of\n\n$$\nf(x) = 2(4)^x+3\n$$\n\nis shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1609/Source/Images/3f864041bbefdf05420e6ff37137cdb2.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186114947 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ee02c223-dd7f-5c5d-b09f-e7bc6bd16b0c" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Root of a Transformed Exponential Function"}] :question/id #uuid "d3c10549-4149-5b51-8f1b-45f807e3cf62" :question/math-academy-id "e-4131" :question/problem "Find the root of $f(x) = 3(2)^{x} - 48$." :question/worked-solution "To find the root of $f(x)$, we must solve\n\n$$\nf(x)=0\n$$\n\n$$\n3(2)^{x} - 48 = 0\n$$\n\nFirst, we isolate the term with the variable exponent by adding $48$ to both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3(2)^{x} - 48 &= 0 \\\\\n3(2)^{x} - 48 + 48 &= 0 + 48 \\\\\n3(2)^{x} &= 48\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDividing both sides by $3$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3(2)^{x} &= 48 \\\\\n2^{x} &= 16\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we rewrite $16$ as $2^4$ to get\n$2^x = 2^4$.\n\nFinally, since both sides of the equation share the same base $(2)$, we equate the exponents. This gives\n$x = 4$.\n\nTherefore, the root of $f(x)$ is $x=4$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186114948 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3c2518d5-0fde-5dfc-aa07-f1bdd3c1585a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the End-Behavior of a Transformed Exponential Function"}] :question/id #uuid "aa011085-4dbf-5688-bd25-f700595f36c3" :question/math-academy-id "e-4132" :question/problem "Suppose that $f(x) = 3(e^{5x}) + 2$. Given that $f(x) \\to a$ as $x \\to -\\infty$, then what is the value of $a$?" :question/worked-solution "Let's sketch the graph of $y=f(x)$:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1609/Source/Images/4f7eca5fb85704c4c86b65a5d83bbab9.png)\n\nSince the function approaches the line $y=2$ as $x\\to -\\infty$, we conclude that\n$f(x)\\to 2 \\quad \\textrm{as}\\quad x\\to -\\infty$.\n\nTherefore, $a=2$.\n\nFinally, we note that the horizontal asymptote of $y=f(x)$ is $y=2$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186114949 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e7df2ec3-6138-5b84-864b-a856c28b9747" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Y-Intercept of a Transformed Exponential Function"}] :question/id #uuid "a8e00166-33ef-5676-9f3c-7f3c85b5fec7" :question/math-academy-id "e-4133" :question/problem "What is the $y$-intercept of $y = -2e^{-2x}+3$?" :question/worked-solution "To find the $y$-intercept, we substitute $x = 0$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= -2e^{-2(0)} + 3 \\\\\n&=-2e^{0} + 3 \\\\\n&=-2(1) + 3 \\\\\n&= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $y$-intercept is $1$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250816 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e7df2ec3-6138-5b84-864b-a856c28b9747" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Y-Intercept of a Transformed Exponential Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f80fcd22-6c2f-54c9-bcea-6585cc2a6b90" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250818} {:answer/id #uuid "b946b83b-9112-5ecf-86c0-767b39353a47" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250819} {:answer/id #uuid "5dcdfb06-b8da-569b-9aef-ea7f80dfd5ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250820} {:answer/id #uuid "951e5ae3-0b1f-5a09-ac84-a573988c4ba1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250821} {:answer/id #uuid "ac344fe6-6ba1-5e22-83fb-eaed1abf3dc0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250822}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ac344fe6-6ba1-5e22-83fb-eaed1abf3dc0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250822} :answer-field/id #uuid "c22b39c0-482e-5ac8-8d31-2d740a2a21db" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250817}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "03234959-d5d6-5dc7-a768-e03667959388" :question/math-academy-id "q-165971" :question/problem "Determine the $y$-intercept of $y={(\\frac{1}{2})}^{5x-2}+1.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the $y$-intercept, we substitute $x=0$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={(\\frac{1}{2})}^{5(0)-2}+1 \\\\  & ={(\\frac{1}{2})}^{-2}+1 \\\\  & ={2}^{2}+1 \\\\  & =4+1 \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $y$-intercept is $5.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250823 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e7df2ec3-6138-5b84-864b-a856c28b9747" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Y-Intercept of a Transformed Exponential Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "beca880c-7b49-5713-9307-bc4e77c73603" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250825} {:answer/id #uuid "51c7df15-5dec-5811-8065-735da190b663" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250826} {:answer/id #uuid "5807e38b-91aa-51b5-a4c5-b4614936ed25" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250827} {:answer/id #uuid "64229d51-14ed-53c3-9f04-2482c9649ed1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250828} {:answer/id #uuid "aad0a18b-ba4c-5de3-94dc-4375cd6c03bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250829}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "51c7df15-5dec-5811-8065-735da190b663" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250826} :answer-field/id #uuid "33e11d04-c046-50db-ad34-70a357cb3d3b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250824}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7b3fbcea-46cf-53b1-8d27-cf3001ea5b83" :question/math-academy-id "q-165972" :question/problem "Determine the $y$-intercept of $y={2}^{-4x+3}-5.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the $y$-intercept, we substitute $x=0$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={2}^{-4(0)+3}-5 \\\\  & ={2}^{3}-5 \\\\  & =8-5 \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $y$-intercept is $3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250830 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ef75bc93-76da-5992-8285-181b808eb7a0" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Range of a Transformed Exponential Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "31939db6-a7a9-5f45-9ab5-1402ddc0c6d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y\\in (-3,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250832} {:answer/id #uuid "ea483945-526f-5e91-8b8d-6b294b4384f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y\\in (-\\infty ,3]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250833} {:answer/id #uuid "97c5412c-33e7-5456-bea1-47e85c6cdcb7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y\\in (-\\infty ,-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250834} {:answer/id #uuid "dbb26809-881f-59cd-9ad7-6ccdbd4919b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y\\in [-3,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250835} {:answer/id #uuid "a06172b2-f368-5bf3-9a97-353158d0113b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y\\in (-\\infty ,3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250836}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "31939db6-a7a9-5f45-9ab5-1402ddc0c6d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y\\in (-3,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250832} :answer-field/id #uuid "b06c741f-03c4-5bc3-b824-7edd41c8c947" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250831}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3b1c999f-be1d-51d5-bb01-040649089426" :question/math-academy-id "q-165973" :question/problem "What is the range of $y=2(5)^{x}-3?$" :question/worked-solution "The range of ${5}^{x}$ is\n\n$$\n{5}^{x}>0.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the above inequality by $2$ and then subtracting $3,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{5}^{x} & >0 \\\\ 2(5)^{x} & >0 \\\\ 2(5)^{x}-3 & >0-3 \\\\ y & >-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the range is $y\\in (-3,\\infty ).$\n\nWe can also see that this is true from the graph of the function. The graph of $f(x)=2(5)^{x}-3$ is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13943819/assets/c8179a12c8032d81ebbdb8a5a95c158e905fab4d24aa68f0eeef1f4bc4971a0b.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250837 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ee02c223-dd7f-5c5d-b09f-e7bc6bd16b0c" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Root of a Transformed Exponential Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5f1a89c0-ab2e-514b-836f-d84f77164b61" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250839} {:answer/id #uuid "2f88ac03-0a84-5ebc-b718-a4ad34dcf9d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250840} {:answer/id #uuid "a59b779a-ac70-560e-915e-b814be89f378" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250841} {:answer/id #uuid "d08b65dd-448b-51ae-acb7-b32f88639ef1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250842} {:answer/id #uuid "97339d77-57c2-550b-b443-f262fb778acd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250843}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5f1a89c0-ab2e-514b-836f-d84f77164b61" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250839} :answer-field/id #uuid "be14988a-21b6-5c88-876b-68312d336e9b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250838}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6cd244e5-78b1-5417-9ff1-33275ef2661d" :question/math-academy-id "q-165974" :question/problem "Find the root of $f(x)=2(5)^{x}-50.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the root of $f(x),$  we must solve $f(x)=0:$\n\n$$\n2(5)^{x}-50=0\n$$\n\nFirst, we isolate the term with the variable exponent by adding $50$ to both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(5)^{x}-50 & =0 \\\\ 2(5)^{x}-\\cancel{50}+\\cancel{50} & =0+50 \\\\ 2(5)^{x} & =50\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDividing both sides by $2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(5)^{x} & =50 \\\\ {5}^{x} & =25.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we rewrite $25$ as ${5}^{2}$ to get\n\n$$\n{5}^{x}={5}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, since both sides of the equation share the same base $(5)$, we equate the exponents. This gives\n\n$$\nx=2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the root of $f(x)$ is $x=2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250844 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ef75bc93-76da-5992-8285-181b808eb7a0" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Range of a Transformed Exponential Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "96c36916-a109-55b8-93cd-1751ba74c634" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y\\in (-\\infty ,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250846} {:answer/id #uuid "0c5c758e-51ba-554f-b5e2-4bd1bd65893b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y\\in (1,+\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250847} {:answer/id #uuid "6233f435-ecaa-5f75-9856-c9ff32da0583" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y\\in (-\\infty ,-1]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250848} {:answer/id #uuid "85c9da1b-b455-561c-848f-9cf1f61b9610" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y\\in (-1,+\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250849} {:answer/id #uuid "ca21d80c-be11-5535-bcad-ad8097238fce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y\\in (-\\infty ,-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250850}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ca21d80c-be11-5535-bcad-ad8097238fce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y\\in (-\\infty ,-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250850} :answer-field/id #uuid "91f58415-3af7-5158-bb7c-c3a5e5fdda97" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250845}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0ba8974b-b12d-5e2d-8293-45498541a65b" :question/math-academy-id "q-40392" :question/problem "What is the range of $y=-3(2)^{2-x}-1?$" :question/worked-solution "The range of ${2}^{-x}$ is \n\n$$\n{2}^{-x}>0.\n$$\n\nHorizontal shifts and stretches do not affect the range. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{2}^{2-x}>0.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the above inequality by $-3$ and then subtracting $1,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{2}^{2-x} & >0 \\\\ -3(2)^{2-x} & <0 \\\\ -3(2)^{2-x}-1 & <0-1 \\\\ y & <-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the range is $y\\in (-\\infty ,-1).$ \n\nWe can also see that this is true from the graph of the function. The graph of $f(x)=-3(2)^{2-x}-1$ is shown below.  \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13943819/assets/88b75fabc306eb09a7ca63c909de3272cc776bf808e29da4cc08ae2c5a53e192.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250851 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ef75bc93-76da-5992-8285-181b808eb7a0" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Range of a Transformed Exponential Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e32709e1-f9be-5b82-9f76-d731a0120ecd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250853} {:answer/id #uuid "18d3f191-98dd-52c2-9ae0-9f3920c7e43a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250854} {:answer/id #uuid "ea4f7e3f-279b-5854-b258-f9e5a8129984" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250855} {:answer/id #uuid "215ea77d-df2f-5ca6-ae93-331ed95a28a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250856} {:answer/id #uuid "861770e4-c455-50cf-87d1-31c8a7efd95e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250857}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "215ea77d-df2f-5ca6-ae93-331ed95a28a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250856} :answer-field/id #uuid "bfa503d1-0f78-5c68-be51-da15744d2f9a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250852}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a8f42f9f-1668-5edc-b773-eb09cabbd64b" :question/math-academy-id "q-40556" :question/problem "Which of the following values ​are possible outputs of $f(x)=-5{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{x-1}+3?$\n\n- $-3$\n- $2$\n- $10$" :question/worked-solution "The range of ${(\\frac{2}{3})}^{x}$ is\n\n$$\n{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{x}>0.\n$$\n\nHorizontal shifts and stretches do not affect the range. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{x-1}>0.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the above inequality by $-5$ and then adding $3,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{x-1} & >0 \\\\ -5{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{x-1} & <0 \\\\ -5{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{x-1}+3 & <0+3 \\\\ f(x) & <3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the range is $f(x)<3.$ Thus, of the given values, only $y=-3$ and $y=2$ are  outputs of $y=f(x).$\n\nWe can also see that this is true from the graph of the function. The graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13943819/assets/b220b9c53bfaa49fe9b1b1c70fb9e21d08e247f44df8716846d08fd37ac2cf11.png)\n\n  \n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250858 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ef75bc93-76da-5992-8285-181b808eb7a0" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Range of a Transformed Exponential Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08f0ce87-d050-5e19-957a-5e5510d9d990" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250860} {:answer/id #uuid "3d7ca28c-b905-56f5-97f2-bc4b2bd63e4a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250861} {:answer/id #uuid "a9d8bb8f-3583-598a-ac0c-fe8ece92b8f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250862} {:answer/id #uuid "6013cc77-c04f-55ca-9423-ddc9a30cb189" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250863} {:answer/id #uuid "1dc45671-fcf7-5dbd-a6f5-ad7bbe7bade5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250864}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1dc45671-fcf7-5dbd-a6f5-ad7bbe7bade5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250864} :answer-field/id #uuid "15e3218f-83b0-52cf-8efc-68875e84a8e5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250859}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "9be6186b-78c0-5e07-9888-65f5a8e9a495" :question/math-academy-id "q-40798" :question/problem "Which of the following values ​are possible outputs of  $f(x)={(1.5)}^{2x}-0.5?$ \n\n- $0$\n- $-0.2$\n- $-0.8$" :question/worked-solution "The range of ${(1.5)}^{x}$ is\n\n$$\n{(1.5)}^{x}>0.\n$$\n\nHorizontal shifts and stretches do not affect the range. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{(1.5)}^{2x}>0.\n$$\n\nSubtracting  $0.5$ from the above inequality yields the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(1.5)}^{2x} & >0 \\\\ {(1.5)}^{2x}-0.5 & >0-0.5 \\\\ f(x) & >-0.5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the range is $f(x)>-0.5.$ Thus, of the given values, only $y=0$ and $y=-0.2$ are outputs of $y=f(x).$\n\nWe can also see that this is true from the graph of the function. The graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13943819/assets/5876d2095bfd91417d2e6395f8a02ae817ebd476076bae929d79709ea7961a3e.png)\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250865 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ef75bc93-76da-5992-8285-181b808eb7a0" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Range of a Transformed Exponential Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b386b797-7dd0-51f1-967b-7999c28d40ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250867} {:answer/id #uuid "cef578bd-2d74-5385-938d-8d34be1e59a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250868} {:answer/id #uuid "ebe2f501-1459-5766-8be9-048735e51c1c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250869} {:answer/id #uuid "e3a9ef1a-73bd-50f4-9852-cc8cdb8812eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250870} {:answer/id #uuid "335ae99e-157a-5998-8a27-132ed22d933b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250871}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ebe2f501-1459-5766-8be9-048735e51c1c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250869} :answer-field/id #uuid "391ead5f-b789-5d3f-a780-f386561cfd8e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250866}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "dee8af6a-2a0f-5055-b4c5-db0b64915d01" :question/math-academy-id "q-40799" :question/problem "Which of the following values ​are possible outputs of $f(x)=-{(2)}^{-x}?$\n\n- $0$\n- $0.5$\n- $-0.5$" :question/worked-solution "The range of ${2}^{-x}$ is\n\n$$\n{2}^{-x}>0.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the above inequality by $-1$ yields the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-1){(2)}^{-x} & <0 \\\\ -{(2)}^{-x} & <0 \\\\ f(x) & <0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the range is $f(x)<0.$ Thus, of the given values, only $y=-0.5$ is an output of $y=f(x).$\n\nWe can also see that this is true from the graph of the function. The graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13943819/assets/95ad3043259402375e7b5822aef88d77027222a3372c2b8ab042d1b54e08666f.png)\n\n \n\nSo, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250872 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ee02c223-dd7f-5c5d-b09f-e7bc6bd16b0c" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Root of a Transformed Exponential Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4c44378a-10aa-52b7-af56-50fc331fee6b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{log}_{2}⁡(3)+2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250874} {:answer/id #uuid "fa29ff71-10ca-584c-8acf-dacb25f21e68" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{log}_{2}⁡(3)-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250875} {:answer/id #uuid "6a6bb87f-b4f8-56b7-b489-2ab782e1657d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3{log}_{2}⁡(4)-1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250876} {:answer/id #uuid "d00fc6bd-5e17-57c4-82d3-32343022514c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4{log}_{2}⁡(3)+4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250877} {:answer/id #uuid "5d6f65f1-e642-5f21-ae3c-aa3ba49bac01" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{log}_{2}⁡(4)-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250878}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d00fc6bd-5e17-57c4-82d3-32343022514c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4{log}_{2}⁡(3)+4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250877} :answer-field/id #uuid "9548eed4-7115-5977-a19f-cb5bbc5a395a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250873}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "62b31fbf-15a4-5f0d-9c03-266e00a60703" :question/math-academy-id "q-66674" :question/problem "Find the root of $f(x)=-{2}^{3x-4}+81.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the root of $f(x),$  we must solve $f(x)=0:$\n\n$$\n-{2}^{3x-4}+81=0\n$$\n\nFirst, we isolate the term with the variable exponent by subtracting $81$ from both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{2}^{3x-4}+81 & =0 \\\\ -{2}^{3x-4}+\\cancel{81}-\\cancel{81} & =0-81 \\\\ -{2}^{3x-4} & =-81 \\\\ {2}^{3x-4} & =81\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we rewrite $81$ as ${3}^{4}$ to get\n\n$$\n{2}^{3x-4}={3}^{4}.\n$$\n\nThen we take ${log}_{2}$ of both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{2}^{3x-4} & ={3}^{4} \\\\ {log}_{2}⁡({2}^{3x-4}) & ={log}_{2}⁡({3}^{4}) \\\\ 3x-4 & =4{log}_{2}⁡(3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x-4 & =4{log}_{2}⁡(3) \\\\ 3x & =4{log}_{2}⁡(3)+4 \\\\ x & =\\frac{4{log}_{2}⁡(3)+4}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250879 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3c2518d5-0fde-5dfc-aa07-f1bdd3c1585a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the End-Behavior of a Transformed Exponential Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "311dc17b-a249-58ee-a96b-10b5b77db289" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250881} {:answer/id #uuid "0695ff2f-582c-53f9-b971-2abdb9ec8d77" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250882} {:answer/id #uuid "e8de1d3b-a3f6-522d-8ebc-c4f6737a4a63" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250883} {:answer/id #uuid "125bbb96-cf8c-505c-8f4d-172b09ec4385" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250884} {:answer/id #uuid "a27074b6-1764-5454-a846-48be72c50cd8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250885}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "125bbb96-cf8c-505c-8f4d-172b09ec4385" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250884} :answer-field/id #uuid "985eee32-097f-5926-bd94-e3e3c9b6691c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250880}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "506ca9f1-32c5-51d1-8d0d-9c0e4b1b5e2a" :question/math-academy-id "q-66684" :question/problem "Suppose that $f(x)=3{(\\frac{1}{5})}^{2x-1}+7.$ Given that $f(x)\\to a$ as $x\\to \\infty ,$ then $a=$" :question/worked-solution "Let's plot the graph of $y=f(x):$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13943819/assets/a3e317bdf1d7c1da85a164331d61dc1500af980ef2b3e907098dd19f42287d8c.png)\n\nSince the function approaches the line $y=7$ as $x\\to \\infty ,$ we conclude that\n\n$$\nf(x)\\to 7\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to \\infty .\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=7.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250886 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e7df2ec3-6138-5b84-864b-a856c28b9747" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Y-Intercept of a Transformed Exponential Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "19d9cb3e-3e2f-5b6c-9d2f-ec73ea9b34a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "There is no $y$-intercept" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250888} {:answer/id #uuid "8b3c7994-b870-52d6-adff-9ff894ecef47" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250889} {:answer/id #uuid "45b512c7-0441-5919-b2d2-3143a0f7fa6f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250890} {:answer/id #uuid "34ab45cd-9954-51f1-bea5-14ae70c93804" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250891} {:answer/id #uuid "3eab864f-37cf-5060-ac22-a7c4e059122f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250892}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "45b512c7-0441-5919-b2d2-3143a0f7fa6f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250890} :answer-field/id #uuid "1644dc10-eb98-5e17-b020-d7143ad7ee24" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250887}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2096fcf5-3e13-58fe-a7ce-4308397f4a9d" :question/math-academy-id "q-92925" :question/problem "Determine the $y$-intercept of $y={3}^{-3x+1}-3.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the $y$-intercept, we substitute $x=0$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={3}^{-3(0)+1}-3 \\\\  & ={3}^{1}-3 \\\\  & =3-3 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $y$-intercept is $0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250893 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e7df2ec3-6138-5b84-864b-a856c28b9747" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Y-Intercept of a Transformed Exponential Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3cd8a0c9-4752-5dbc-9ef5-1eb6be87880b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250895} {:answer/id #uuid "1b4295bd-4edf-5d0c-a99f-aa3651f283be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{101}{100}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250896} {:answer/id #uuid "269e75eb-b53c-5d4a-ae29-05fbc52afcb9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "There is no $y$-intercept" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250897} {:answer/id #uuid "222fe4e0-5900-52cd-894b-49747b216c5a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250898} {:answer/id #uuid "ec9be228-b251-53db-9abf-16e69436d6da" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250899}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "222fe4e0-5900-52cd-894b-49747b216c5a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250898} :answer-field/id #uuid "b3e1a0e3-8424-554e-851b-6a1d37bb03ec" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250894}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1ac0fce3-540d-53f6-ae4b-b41d1a5a4d86" :question/math-academy-id "q-92929" :question/problem "Determine the $y$-intercept of $y={(\\frac{1}{10})}^{2x}+1.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the $y$-intercept, we substitute $x=0$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={(\\frac{1}{10})}^{2(0)}+1 \\\\  & ={(\\frac{1}{10})}^{0}+1 \\\\  & =1+1 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $y$-intercept is $2.$"}] [{