infty ,$ we conclude that the equation of the horizontal asymptote is $y=-5.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250935 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ee02c223-dd7f-5c5d-b09f-e7bc6bd16b0c" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Root of a Transformed Exponential Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8819b3ba-cf8b-5860-b897-e8e0c51668e7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250937} {:answer/id #uuid "b2473fab-db0b-5ba0-817f-c33a6f9c697f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250938} {:answer/id #uuid "9872287a-d14b-572e-b283-a073b595b927" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250939} {:answer/id #uuid "e339b207-78ee-50fa-b0cb-06d77b8ea048" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250940} {:answer/id #uuid "4efff487-16cb-59f5-87e5-4bfe5cb45662" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250941}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e339b207-78ee-50fa-b0cb-06d77b8ea048" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250940} :answer-field/id #uuid "cfa08790-b879-579a-a81f-5cf216affe71" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250936}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f6bb98f4-14ff-5456-8b32-1fb3e17bc5b9" :question/math-academy-id "q-92995" :question/problem "Find the root of $f(x)={e}^{x}-{e}^{4}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the root of $f(x),$  we must solve $f(x)=0:$\n\n$$\n{e}^{x}-{e}^{4}=0\n$$\n\nFirst, we isolate the term with the variable exponent by adding ${e}^{4}$ to both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x}-{e}^{4} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{x}-\\cancel{{e}^{4}}+\\cancel{{e}^{4}} & =0+{e}^{4} \\\\ {e}^{x} & ={e}^{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since both sides of the equation share the same base $e$, we equate the exponents. This gives\n\n$$\nx=4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the root of $f(x)$ is $x=4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250942 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ee02c223-dd7f-5c5d-b09f-e7bc6bd16b0c" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Root of a Transformed Exponential Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4aa962d8-2359-5b0b-bc47-6962a02a3d51" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)-1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250944} {:answer/id #uuid "aff65057-e752-5d86-a8d7-29ab66bcb91a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)+1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250945} {:answer/id #uuid "ca1a10dc-ece8-5f93-bbfd-2798c9d2c2c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250946} {:answer/id #uuid "1b2d2fd1-765b-5f2f-bd4d-e98802629809" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250947} {:answer/id #uuid "fec070c8-59e3-58f6-a7aa-62488f62c58a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250948}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "aff65057-e752-5d86-a8d7-29ab66bcb91a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)+1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250945} :answer-field/id #uuid "b1a89f27-0e4d-5982-b3d8-98c60e5f4cca" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250943}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "bee910a5-bfec-5da0-b294-9b388e560648" :question/math-academy-id "q-92998" :question/problem "Find the root of $f(x)=4{e}^{2x-1}-8.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the root of $f(x),$  we must solve $f(x)=0:$\n\n$$\n4{e}^{2x-1}-8=0\n$$\n\nFirst, we isolate the term with the variable exponent by adding $8$ to both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{e}^{2x-1}-8 & =0 \\\\ 4{e}^{2x-1}-\\cancel{8}+\\cancel{8} & =0+8 \\\\ 4{e}^{2x-1} & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDividing both sides by $4,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{e}^{2x-1} & =8 \\\\ {e}^{2x-1} & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThen we take $ln$ of both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2x-1} & =2 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{2x-1}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2) \\\\ 2x-1 & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-1 & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2) \\\\ 2x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)+1 \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)+1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250949 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ee02c223-dd7f-5c5d-b09f-e7bc6bd16b0c" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Root of a Transformed Exponential Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c8af98a8-a5da-5ef4-954a-fa8c6e8a8995" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250951} {:answer/id #uuid "8eea48af-6294-5306-95c6-a4b5d8fe9d5d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250952} {:answer/id #uuid "769bd808-4940-5dc2-8e70-4cb9b444587d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250953} {:answer/id #uuid "542cc09b-b9e2-56a9-8c38-b618e006ac6d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250954} {:answer/id #uuid "6b55e90c-4cba-5c40-a2f2-87e034c50df0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250955}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "769bd808-4940-5dc2-8e70-4cb9b444587d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250953} :answer-field/id #uuid "e2611de0-f2bd-5f2a-b92f-3ca250a6e87e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250950}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "dfe344bf-34e4-55e9-b8e1-a7b9ffb4f282" :question/math-academy-id "q-93000" :question/problem "Find the root of $f(x)=-15{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{x-1}+10.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the root of $f(x),$  we must solve $f(x)=0:$\n\n$$\n-15{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{x-1}+10=0\n$$\n\nFirst, we isolate the term with the variable exponent by subtracting $10$ from both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-15{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{x-1}+10 & =0 \\\\ -15{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{x-1}+\\cancel{10}-\\cancel{10} & =0-10 \\\\ -15{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{x-1} & =-10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDividing both sides by $-15,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-15{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{x-1} & =-10 \\\\ {(\\frac{3}{2})}^{x-1} & =\\frac{10}{15} \\\\ {(\\frac{3}{2})}^{x-1} & =\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we rewrite $\\frac{2}{3}$ as ${(\\frac{3}{2})}^{-1}$ to get\n\n$$\n{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{x-1}={(\\frac{3}{2})}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nFinally, since both sides of the equation share the same base $(\\frac{3}{2})$, we equate the exponents. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-1 & =-1 \\\\ x & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the root of $f(x)$ is $x=0.$"}]}
